_slug":"sarah-hanson-young","vote":"aye"},{"name":"Sean Bell","party":"One Nation","person_id":"tvfy_11062","person_slug":"sean-bell","vote":"no"},{"name":"Steph Hodgins-May","party":"Greens","person_id":"tvfy_11020","person_slug":"steph-hodgins-may","vote":"aye"},{"name":"Tammy Tyrrell","party":"Independent","person_id":"tvfy_11012","person_slug":"tammy-tyrrell","vote":"no"},{"name":"Tony Sheldon","party":"Labor","person_id":"tvfy_10944","person_slug":"tony-sheldon","vote":"no"},{"name":"Tyron Whitten","party":"One Nation","person_id":"tvfy_11060","person_slug":"tyron-whitten","vote":"no"},{"name":"Varun Ghosh","party":"Labor","person_id":"tvfy_11017","person_slug":"varun-ghosh","vote":"no"},{"name":"Wendy Askew","party":"Liberal","person_id":"tvfy_10914","person_slug":"wendy-askew","vote":"no"}],"name":"Matters of Urgency — Gas Industry: Taxation","noes":30,"question":"Slade Brockman Senator McKim has submitted a proposal, under standing order 75, today. It is shown at item 12 of today's Order of Business: Pursuant to standing order 75, I give notice that today the Australian Greens propose to move \"That, in the opinion of the Senate, the following is a matter of urgency: The need for the Australian Government to place a moratorium on meetings with representatives of the gas industry and their lobbyists for the duration of the public debate on the taxation of gas exports, revoke sponsored parliamentary passes held by such representatives, and immediately disclose all meetings held between Government Ministers and representatives of the gas industry or their lobbyists over the previous 12 months.\" Is consideration of the proposal supported? _More than the number of senators required by the standing orders having risen in their places—_ With the concurrence of the Senate, the clerks will set the clock in line with the informal arrangements made by the whips. Steph Hodgins-May I move: That, in the opinion of the Senate, the following is a matter of urgency: The need for the Australian Government to place a moratorium on meetings with representatives of the gas industry and their lobbyists for the duration of the public debate on the taxation of gas exports, revoke sponsored parliamentary passes held by such representatives, and immediately disclose all meetings held between Government Ministers and representatives of the gas industry or their lobbyists over the previous 12 months. The corporate parties have given the gas lobby free run in this building for far too long, and the Greens are saying time is up. Australians have a simple message for the Albanese government: stop the great Australian gas rip-off and make corporations pay what they owe. During our Greens led gas tax inquiry, we heard it over and over again from Shell, INPEX, AEP, Woodside—different logos, same script: 'The PRRT is working. Now isn't the time for more taxes.","result":"negative","slug":"division-federal-senate-10274","source_url":"https://theyvoteforyou.org.au/divisions/senate/2026-06-22/6"}
